  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 346 AM EDT Tue Aug 18 2026\n\nChances for unsettled weather return Wednesday as multiple \nshortwaves move towards the Ohio and Mississippi valleys. The first \nwave will slowly diminish as it enters the region, resulting in low \nbut non-zero chances for showers and an isolated storm. Per the \nlatest HREF, chances of precipitation through the mid-afternoon \nhours range from 10-25% over southern Indiana and north central \nKentucky. This is in part due to the combination of limited CAPE \n(<500 J/kg) and wind shear (20-30 kt 0-6 km shear) at that time. \nLooking broadly across the area on Wednesday, most places will \nremain dry, especially for locations south of I-64 and east of I-65. \nTemperatures will be slightly warmer than Tuesday, with highs in the \nlow to mid 80s north of the Parkways and in the upper 80s/around 90 \nsouth.\n\nThe second shortwave will move towards the region Wednesday night, \nalong with a weak surface frontal boundary that will slowly push \nsouthward into Thursday. As a result, widespread showers and \nthunderstorms are forecast Wednesday night through Thursday morning \n(50-70% chance). Accompanying these features will be a modest low-\nlevel jet (30-40 kt) that will help produce 30-40 kt of deep-layer \nshear. Plentiful moisture will also be in place ahead of the frontal \nboundary, with LREF outputting >1.8\" of PW. Since this event will \noccur later in the overnight, instability will be rather meager and \nlimit the potential for severe weather. \n\nBy late Thursday, the upper-level trough will dig into the Ohio \nValley, with the surface frontal boundary pushing off to our south. \nChances for showers and storms will linger into the late evening \nhours Thursday as the front slowly moves through Kentucky. While \nmodest instability (~1500 J/kg of SBCAPE) will be present, a \nweakening low-level jet will keep chances for stronger storms to \ndevelop on the lower end. Once the upper trough digs over the area \nby Friday, surface high pressure will fill in over the region, \nallowing for a comfortable and dry end to the workweek. As the axis \nof the upper trough swings over the region late Saturday into early \nSunday, another round of showers is possible. However, with fairly \ndry air in place, chances will remain low (<15%). For the remainder \nof the weekend and the start of the next workweek, expect dry and \npleasant, with afternoon temperatures staying in the 80s across the \narea.",
